VERDICT: Japanese is not yet robust enough for Foothold's basic-reading-competency goal.
The curriculum has breadth, but the path is weakened by script/kanji misalignment and broken grammar gating.
Top gap 1: Grammar drills do not mark grammar orders known, so scenes/readings/checkpoints are not coherently unlocked.
Top gap 2: Kanji course is not aligned with lexicon/readings; readings use 71 kanji outside the 317-kanji set.
Top gap 3: Kanji examples are not linted and include 214 example words outside the lexicon.
Top gap 4: Kana lacks explicit small tsu, long-vowel mark, long-vowel spelling, and foreign katakana combinations before vocab uses them.
Top gap 5: Practical sign/message vocabulary is missing or late: 本日休業, 営業中, 注意, 危険, 非常口, 受付, レシート, ホーム, 遅延.
Core vocabulary is broad but spends late units on lower-priority specialty nouns before common public-text phrases.
Grammar inventory is sizable, but missing/weak items include や, ね/よ, quote と, ないでください, noun modifiers, 時 clauses, and の nominalization.
SRS is serviceable, and content lint passes for checked text, but lint does not cover kanji examples or script-surface prerequisites.
The readings are useful controlled practice, but they do not yet prove transfer to everyday Japanese signs/messages.
Full review written to docs/japanese-curriculum-review.md.
